llows cloud escalation only through the `escalate_to_frontier` tool \u2014 which is **user-initiated and attended-only**. This is a deliberate boundary: the supervisor never makes autonomous decisions to route user requests to cloud models.\n\nThe proposed change introduces **supervisor-initiated automatic escalation** driven by the local reasoner's confidence score. This directly contradicts two parts of ADR-ARCH-001:\n\n1. **The local-first hot-path invariant** \u2014 the supervisor would no longer be the sole decision-maker in the hot path for safety-critical requests; a cloud model would be invoked automatically without user consent.\n2. **The attended-only constraint** \u2014 cloud escalation would become an internal mechanism rather than an explicit user choice, removing the human-in-the-loop gate.\n\n### What would need to change\n\n- **ADR-ARCH-001** would need to be amended (or a new ADR created) to redefine the \"attended-only\" boundary \u2014 explicitly permitting supervisor-triggered cloud escalation under bounded, auditable conditions (e.g., confidence thresholds, budget caps, safety-critical classification).\n- **The supervisor's contract** would need to be updated to define:\n  - What constitutes \"low confidence\" (measurable signal from the local reasoner?).\n  - What qualifies as \"safety-critical\" or \"high-stakes\" (classification taxonomy?).\n  - How the per-session budget cap is enforced and audited.\n  - Whether the user is notified before the escalation fires (partial attended-ness).\n\nWithout these changes, the proposal sits outside ADR-ARCH-001's design envelope. The architect's judgment is clear: **this is a design-level change, not a small tweak** \u2014 it would require an ADR update before implementation.\nUSER: Now please start a GCSE English Literature tutoring session on Macbeth, focused on AO1 and AO2 (knowledge of the text and language analysis), then ask the tutor to walk me through how Shakespeare uses imagery to characterise 